## 7. Section 5 — Risk & Attribution Analytics (`utils.risk_analytics`)

All helpers operate on **decimal** returns. Annualisation uses `TRADING_DAYS = 252`.

### MCTR — `compute_risk_contributions(weights, cov)`
Euler decomposition of portfolio volatility:
- `σ_p = √(w' Σ w)`
- `MCTR_i = (Σ w)_i / σ_p`
- `CCTR_i = w_i × MCTR_i` (component contributions sum to `σ_p`)
- `% of Total = CCTR_i / σ_p` (sums to 1)

### MCTE — `compute_active_risk_contributions(w_p, w_b, cov)`
Same idea on `w_active = w_p − w_b` over the **union** of tickers (zeros fill the rest):
- `TE = √(w_active' Σ w_active)`
- `CCTE_i = w_active_i × MCTE_i` (sums to TE)

### VaR / CVaR — `compute_var_cvar(returns, confidence_levels, horizon_days, annualise)`
- `α = 1 − CL`. Historical VaR = `quantile(α)`; Historical CVaR = mean of returns ≤ VaR.
- Parametric Gaussian h-day: `VaR = μh + σ√h × z`, `CVaR = μh − σ√h × φ(z) / α`.
- `annualise=True` ↔ `horizon_days = 252` (mutually exclusive overrides). The UI checkbox label and help text reflect this exactly.

### Drawdown Episodes — `compute_drawdown_episodes(returns, top_n)`
- NAV `(1+r).cumprod()`; drawdown `NAV/cummax − 1`.
- Peak Date = the day before drawdown started. Trough Date = `idxmin` of the episode segment. Recovery Date = first day NAV regains the prior peak; `pd.NaT` + `Ongoing=True` if no recovery.
- Top-N sorted by depth (most negative first).

### Up/Down Capture — `compute_capture_ratios(p, b, frequency)`
- Aggregate to monthly/quarterly/annual via geometric compound `(1+x).prod() − 1`. Empty-bucket rows are dropped (no fake 0% periods).
- Up periods = `b > 0`, down = `b < 0`.
- Up Capture = `geo_mean(p_up) / geo_mean(b_up)`.
- Hit Ratio = `(p > b).mean()` (strict beat). Batting Average = `(p ≥ b).mean()` (≥ tie).

### Brinson — `compute_brinson_attribution(w_p, w_b, period_returns, exposure_table)`
**Brinson-Fachler** (allocation deducts benchmark total):
- Bucket weight: `w_p_b = Σ_i w_p_i × exp_{i,b}` (decimal exposures).
- Bucket return: `R_p_b = Σ_i (w_p_i × exp_{i,b} × r_i) / Σ_i (w_p_i × exp_{i,b})` — look-through approximation when only ETF-level returns are available.
- `Allocation_b = (w_p_b − w_b_b) × (R_b_b − R_b_total)`
- `Selection_b  =  w_b_b × (R_p_b − R_b_b)`
- `Interaction_b = (w_p_b − w_b_b) × (R_p_b − R_b_b)`
- `Σ_b (Alloc + Sel + Inter) = active_return` exactly (model active).
- **The UI tab uses time-averaged daily weights** for the portfolio (mean of daily weights in the chosen period), shows realised port/bench/active cards alongside the Brinson decomposition with a **Residual** = realised_active − Brinson_active. The residual captures rebalancing/drift effects a single-period model can't represent.
- Excel export uses the same time-averaged weights for consistency.
- When the user picks a non-USD display currency, `rets_period` gets the same `(1+R_USD)(1+R_FX) − 1` overlay so realised cards and Brinson reconcile in the displayed currency.

### Efficient Frontier — `compute_efficient_frontier(returns_df, n_points)`
- Long-only, no leverage: `w ∈ [0, 1]`, `Σw = 1`.
- Mean & cov annualised by ×252.
- Sweep target return between `min_ret` (global min variance) and `max(μ)` (max single-asset return). For each target solve `min w'Σw` s.t. `μ'w = target`.
- `evaluate_portfolio_on_frontier(weights, returns_df)` overlays the actual portfolio. UI tab uses the same `ef_returns` panel for the curve, asset dots, and the portfolio/benchmark dots so all three are mutually comparable.

